<!DOCTYPE html><html><head><title>IRC channel logs</title><style>html {
  background: #fdfdfd;
}

h1 {
  font-weight: 300;
}

h2 {
  font-weight: 200;
}

h3 {
  padding: .5em 0;
  border-top: 3px dotted #ddd;
  margin-bottom: 0;
}

form {
  width: 400px;
  display: flex;
}

input {
  width: 100%;
  display: flex;
  border-radius: .25em 0 0 .25em;
  border: 1px solid #aaa;
  border-right: 0;
  padding: 0.5em;
}

button {
  display: flex;
  border-radius: 0 .25em .25em 0;
  background-color: #007bff;
  border: 1px solid #007bff;
  padding: .5em;
  cursor: pointer;
  color: white;
}

button:hover {
  background-color: #0069d9;
  border-color: #0062cc;
}

a {
  color: #007bff;
  text-decoration: none;
}

a:hover {
  text-decoration: underline;
}

h4 {
  margin-bottom: .5em;
}

table td {
  padding: 0.75em;
}

table tr:hover {
  background: #eee;
}

.year {
  display: table;
}

.month {
  display: table-cell;
  padding-right: 1em;
}

ul {
  margin: 0;
  padding: 0;
  list-style: none;
}

.nick {
  padding-right: 0.6rem;
  font-weight: bold;
  text-align: right;
  width: 13rem;
  display: table-cell;
}

.nick a {
  color: inherit;
  text-decoration: none;
}

.message {
  display: table-cell;
  padding-left: 0.6rem;
  border-left: 2px solid #333;
}

.notice {
  color: #859900;
  font-style: italic;
}

.line {
  line-height: 1.8rem;
  display: table;
}

#logs {
  margin-top: 1.5rem;
  padding: 1.5rem;
}
</style></head><body><h1>IRC channel logs</h1><h2>2024-12-14.log</h2><p><a href="/bootstrappable">back to list of logs</a></p><div id="logs"><div class="line" id="001328"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#001328" label="[00:13:28]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">non stable downloads is probably one of the most annoying things that affected us in live-bootstrap</span></div><div class="line" id="001335"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#001335" label="[00:13:35]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and it happens over and over again...</span></div><div class="line" id="001349"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#001349" label="[00:13:49]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">it's not the same project, sometimes it was openssl, now savannah...</span></div><div class="line" id="004139"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#004139" label="[00:41:39]">&lt;temp64&gt;</a></span><span class="message">totally abusing ftp.gnu.org <a rel="nofollow" href="https://paste.gentoo.zip/Omos4Hmi">https://paste.gentoo.zip/Omos4Hmi</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="004250"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#004250" label="[00:42:50]">&lt;temp64&gt;</a></span><span class="message">going to build all of that tomorrow</span></div><div class="line" id="004352"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#004352" label="[00:43:52]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">most of the sources are set to mirrors.kernel.org, not ftp.gnu.org</span></div><div class="line" id="124332"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#124332" label="[12:43:32]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">meanwhile, sanitized musl-libc to exclude functions and syscalls which linux-2.4 does not support</span></div><div class="line" id="124403"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#124403" label="[12:44:03]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">that is 200 syscalls less than what linux got nowadays, and this caused almost no regressions against those ~500builds that i maintain for support with tinycc</span></div><div class="line" id="124609"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#124609" label="[12:46:09]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">there's only a few troublemakers, introduced to avoid race-conditions with thread support from kernel, pselect() for example and openat with O_CLOEXEC support</span></div><div class="line" id="124653"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#124653" label="[12:46:53]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">nonetheless, for kernel-bootstrapping the tcc/musl/linux-2.4 seems a decent approach, to emit a complete distribution even</span></div><div class="line" id="125056"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#125056" label="[12:50:56]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">since when musl-libc got sanitized last night, almost all regressions of linux-2.4 ABI and musl should be revealed compile-time now, and hopefully there won't be too many sporadic run-time ones</span></div><div class="line" id="125138"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#125138" label="[12:51:38]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">a few quick initial tests, links2 -g browser works already, mutt mailer, busybox etc. etc.; that's heavyweights which imply broad test coverage passed</span></div><div class="line" id="125307"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#125307" label="[12:53:07]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">with a tinycc compiled musl-libc/linux-2.4</span></div><div class="line" id="125417"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#125417" label="[12:54:17]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">hence for bootstrapping it is possible to fork a complete distribution as soon as tinycc and musl-libc are available, without bootstrapping any later kernel version nor gcc etc.</span></div><div class="line" id="125524"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#125524" label="[12:55:24]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">much less lines of code, much less compile-time, and _more_ software available, including gdb, strace etc.</span></div><div class="line" id="130139"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#130139" label="[13:01:39]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">is linux 2.4 easiest of all kernels to start bootstrap with? what about hurd/mach?</span></div><div class="line" id="130217"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#130217" label="[13:02:17]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">nope, the tinycc/linux-2.4/musl-libc certainly isn't the &quot;easiest&quot;, but it seems the most practical one hitting the sweet spot</span></div><div class="line" id="130300"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#130300" label="[13:03:00]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">in between absent hardware support (absent usb with fiwix/munix/mach etc) and the excess software-bloat common nowadays</span></div><div class="line" id="130330"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#130330" label="[13:03:30]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">furthermore, linux-2.4/musl-libc provide a sufficiently complete syscall/posix api/abi for recent software versions</span></div><div class="line" id="130847"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#130847" label="[13:08:47]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Andrius Štikonas&gt; homo: that's not really a correct question, you don't need linux 2.4 to bootstrap</span></div><div class="line" id="130934"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#130934" label="[13:09:34]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Andrius Štikonas&gt; what do you want to bootstrap? userspace: modern kernel is the easiest. kernel: you should start with something really simple such as builder-hex0 and then build Fiwix kernel</span></div><div class="line" id="130957"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#130957" label="[13:09:57]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;Andrius Štikonas&gt; then you can build Linux kernel from there</span></div><div class="line" id="131023"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#131023" label="[13:10:23]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yes, but linux-2.x can be integrated into a clean bootstrapping path, _and_ it provides sufficient features for a somewhat complete distribution rather early within the bootstrapping path with far less dependency clutter</span></div><div class="line" id="131035"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#131035" label="[13:10:35]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">than a modern linux-kernel and gcc would need</span></div><div class="line" id="131127"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#131127" label="[13:11:27]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I don't think I agree with what is clean bootstrapping path. what is not clean about live-bootstrap</span></div><div class="line" id="131204"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#131204" label="[13:12:04]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">live-bootstrap seems fine, i see problems with gcc and recent kernel versions</span></div><div class="line" id="131205"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#131205" label="[13:12:05]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">more dependencies, perhaps, but I wouldn't call it unclean</span></div><div class="line" id="132108"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#132108" label="[13:21:08]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">after many hours of reading source code of hugs, I don't understand it at all, I really hoped this tiny patch was enough to have bangpatterns just so that I don't need to patch them out of microhs <a rel="nofollow" href="https://paste.debian.net/1339807/">https://paste.debian.net/1339807/</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="132221"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#132221" label="[13:22:21]">&lt;homo&gt;</a></span><span class="message">it should be possible to bootstrap microhs from hugs as it uses extensions available in hugs</span></div><div class="line" id="182729"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#182729" label="[18:27:29]">&lt;rekado&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Rutherther: thanks for the hints!  It works now.  I think I have enough information together to write a Cookbook entry.</span></div><div class="line" id="182822"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#182822" label="[18:28:22]">&lt;rekado&gt;</a></span><span class="message">(though perhaps we should consider adding a window-manager-service-type as an alternative to desktop-service-type)</span></div><div class="line" id="190240"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#190240" label="[19:02:40]">&lt;temp64&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Hi, I tried running live-bootstrap in an alpine container but something ain't right: <a rel="nofollow" href="https://paste.gentoo.zip/MiMQLLNT">https://paste.gentoo.zip/MiMQLLNT</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="190356"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#190356" label="[19:03:56]">&lt;temp64&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I copied all files from the root of the repository like so: <a rel="nofollow" href="https://paste.gentoo.zip/rBPe324t">https://paste.gentoo.zip/rBPe324t</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="190602"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#190602" label="[19:06:02]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">rootfs.py also creates configuration file I think (bootstrap.cfg)</span></div><div class="line" id="190604"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#190604" label="[19:06:04]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">do you have it?</span></div><div class="line" id="190727"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#190727" label="[19:07:27]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">temp64: it should look someting like <a rel="nofollow" href="https://paste.debian.net/1339856/">https://paste.debian.net/1339856/</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="190803"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#190803" label="[19:08:03]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">temp64: also keep in mind, that only x86 bootstrap works now</span></div><div class="line" id="190824"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#190824" label="[19:08:24]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">amd64 version is not complete</span></div><div class="line" id="190834"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#190834" label="[19:08:34]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">mescc-&gt;tcc step is still buggy and crashes</span></div><div class="line" id="190857"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#190857" label="[19:08:57]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">we can build very first tcc binary, but it is only partially functional</span></div><div class="line" id="190916"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#190916" label="[19:09:16]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">See <a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/fosslinux/live-bootstrap/issues/470">https://github.com/fosslinux/live-bootstrap/issues/470</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="190941"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#190941" label="[19:09:41]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">riscv64 actually is in a better state than amd64...</span></div><div class="line" id="191000"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#191000" label="[19:10:00]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">though riscv64 work also helped with some amd64 bugs, it goes a bit further on amd64 now than before</span></div><div class="line" id="191006"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#191006" label="[19:10:06]">&lt;temp64&gt;</a></span><span class="message">oh</span></div><div class="line" id="191051"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#191051" label="[19:10:51]">&lt;temp64&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I wish I had known that earlier</span></div><div class="line" id="191202"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#191202" label="[19:12:02]">&lt;temp64&gt;</a></span><span class="message">can't you just cross-compile amd64 binaries after x86 bootstrap though?</span></div><div class="line" id="191343"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#191343" label="[19:13:43]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">sure, that's what I have done for my current OS</span></div><div class="line" id="191414"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#191414" label="[19:14:14]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">generally, the bootstrap path should be more or less the same</span></div><div class="line" id="191424"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#191424" label="[19:14:24]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">it's just various bugs that are preventing it</span></div><div class="line" id="191809"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#191809" label="[19:18:09]">&lt;rekado&gt;</a></span><span class="message">ACTION wrote to the wrong channel, oops</span></div><div class="line" id="191858"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#191858" label="[19:18:58]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">temp64: tinycc and fiwix do not support x86_64 to keep it brief, and those are crucial to bootstrapping</span></div><div class="line" id="191941"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#191941" label="[19:19:41]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">tinycc does support x86_64, only fiwix doesn't</span></div><div class="line" id="192005"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#192005" label="[19:20:05]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">stikonas: last time i tried (2 years ago) significant parts for 64bit asm processing were absent</span></div><div class="line" id="192012"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#192012" label="[19:20:12]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">oh asm maybe</span></div><div class="line" id="192018"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#192018" label="[19:20:18]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but asm is usually trivial to add</span></div><div class="line" id="192026"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#192026" label="[19:20:26]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">well, for me it isn't</span></div><div class="line" id="192028"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#192028" label="[19:20:28]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">ekaitz added lots of opcodes to tcc</span></div><div class="line" id="192104"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#192104" label="[19:21:04]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and i feel urged to respond to another thing</span></div><div class="line" id="192105"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#192105" label="[19:21:05]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">2024-12-14 12:12:05 &lt;stikonas&gt; more dependencies, perhaps, but I wouldn't call it unclean</span></div><div class="line" id="192144"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#192144" label="[19:21:44]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">assembly opcode is just some sequence of bits</span></div><div class="line" id="192150"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#192150" label="[19:21:50]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">so you just emit those bits into binary</span></div><div class="line" id="192207"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#192207" label="[19:22:07]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">^ i didn't call live-bootstrap nor kernel unclean</span></div><div class="line" id="192208"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#192208" label="[19:22:08]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">(plus the bits corresponding to registers and / or immediates)</span></div><div class="line" id="192241"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#192241" label="[19:22:41]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">so assembly is really far far easier to implement than any compiler</span></div><div class="line" id="192315"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#192315" label="[19:23:15]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">nonetheless assembly support for any architecture is signifcant efforts, finally bundled into binutils, and rewriting this isn't trivial</span></div><div class="line" id="192351"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#192351" label="[19:23:51]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">well, binutils have far better coverage of assembly</span></div><div class="line" id="192354"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#192354" label="[19:23:54]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">that's true</span></div><div class="line" id="192419"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#192419" label="[19:24:19]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but that's because everybody uses binutils, if instruction is not in binutils, nobody would be using that instruction</span></div><div class="line" id="192443"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#192443" label="[19:24:43]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">it's both a blessing all that got implemented with binutils, and it's too a burden</span></div><div class="line" id="192530"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#192530" label="[19:25:30]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">anyway, i didn't call live-bootstrap unclean, never</span></div><div class="line" id="195712"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#195712" label="[19:57:12]">&lt;ekaitz&gt;</a></span><span class="message">aggi: i can add more if you want</span></div><div class="line" id="200437"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#200437" label="[20:04:37]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">ekaitz: it's x86_32 in this case, but some advice would be appreciated</span></div><div class="line" id="200438"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#200438" label="[20:04:38]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message"> <a rel="nofollow" href="https://codeberg.org/aggi/linux-tcc/commit/b3e38adb647cd6e0de0fb8287e654bf3752c9358">https://codeberg.org/aggi/linux-tcc/commit/b3e38adb647cd6e0de0fb8287e654bf3752c9358</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="200455"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#200455" label="[20:04:55]">&lt;ekaitz&gt;</a></span><span class="message">a x86 is not really my thing... i added riscv things only</span></div><div class="line" id="201200"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#201200" label="[20:12:00]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">ekaitz: i've not played with some risc-v, i merely hope it's dependency graph won't blow up like the arm u-boot one did (attaching python/swig/c++ build-time dependency to the boot-loader)</span></div><div class="line" id="201230"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#201230" label="[20:12:30]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">because if it did, bootstrapping u-boot loader got blocked</span></div><div class="line" id="201837"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#201837" label="[20:18:37]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">if &gt;gcc-4.7.4/g++ cannot be avoided with any kernel version needed for risc-v, this too would trap risc-v bootstrapping onto x86 bootstrapping/cross-compilation/</span></div><div class="line" id="201936"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#201936" label="[20:19:36]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">to my knowledge it's difficult to compile/link/assemble any later kernel version than linux-2.4 (or fiwix) with tcc (regardless of the ISA)</span></div><div class="line" id="202412"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#202412" label="[20:24:12]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">furthermore, Intel is phasing out x86_32 UEFI/CSM support; with the consequence of x86 hardware being available with a somewhat clean bootstrapping path cannot boot 16bit real-mode anylonger then</span></div><div class="line" id="202504"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#202504" label="[20:25:04]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">that's triple the fun, and quardrupled if all that remained was qemu, where some discussion indicated rust-lang creeped into too,</span></div><div class="line" id="202515"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#202515" label="[20:25:15]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">that's fivefold fun with bootstrapping then</span></div><div class="line" id="202553"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#202553" label="[20:25:53]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message">For riscv boards which aren't meant to be microcontrollers you should only need an uefi bootloader. Could have the question of the boot firmware that's on a dedicated chip but you'd need to consider coreboot for x86 then.</span></div><div class="line" id="212243"><span class="nick" style="color:#d9d9d9"><a href="#212243" label="[21:22:43]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">rekado: did you intended to post that here, or in #guix ?</span></div><div class="line" id="222959"><span class="nick" style="color:#bc80bd"><a href="#222959" label="[22:29:59]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">hmmm, i recently redownloaded gnulibs, so this must have been in the last few weeks</span></div><div class="line" id="223005"><span class="nick" style="color:#bc80bd"><a href="#223005" label="[22:30:05]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">ugh, that is really annoying</span></div><div class="line" id="223120"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#223120" label="[22:31:20]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">indeed...</span></div><div class="line" id="223129"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#223129" label="[22:31:29]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">we could predownload gnulibs with git and export locally</span></div><div class="line" id="223542"><span class="nick" style="color:#bc80bd"><a href="#223542" label="[22:35:42]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Googulator already did that in commit 25443468</span></div><div class="line" id="223553"><span class="nick" style="color:#bc80bd"><a href="#223553" label="[22:35:53]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">preemptively back in April</span></div><div class="line" id="223629"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#223629" label="[22:36:29]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yeah, that helps a bit</span></div><div class="line" id="223658"><span class="nick" style="color:#bc80bd"><a href="#223658" label="[22:36:58]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message"> <a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/coreutils/gnulib">https://github.com/coreutils/gnulib</a>  could use this?</span></div><div class="line" id="223707"><span class="nick" style="color:#bc80bd"><a href="#223707" label="[22:37:07]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">idk which i prefer...</span></div><div class="line" id="223721"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#223721" label="[22:37:21]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">maybe we could...</span></div><div class="line" id="223759"><span class="nick" style="color:#bc80bd"><a href="#223759" label="[22:37:59]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">we always have files.bootstrapping.world as a backup</span></div><div class="line" id="223806"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#223806" label="[22:38:06]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">true</span></div><div class="line" id="224518"><span class="nick" style="color:#bc80bd"><a href="#224518" label="[22:45:18]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">painfully, the github tarballs are not identical to the cgit tarballs, because github includes the full commit hash</span></div><div class="line" id="225018"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#225018" label="[22:50:18]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">how about other forges?</span></div><div class="line" id="225027"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#225027" label="[22:50:27]">&lt;stikonas&gt;</a></span><span class="message">codeberg? gitlab?</span></div><div class="line" id="225903"><span class="nick" style="color:#3c5b35"><a href="#225903" label="[22:59:03]">&lt;deesix&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Forge's autogenerated archives are a lottery. Are we so quickly forgetting, for example, <a rel="nofollow" href="https://lwn.net/Articles/921787/">https://lwn.net/Articles/921787/</a>  ?</span></div><div class="line" id="225948"><span class="nick" style="color:#3c5b35"><a href="#225948" label="[22:59:48]">&lt;deesix&gt;</a></span><span class="message">(changes in compression config giving different archives)</span></div><div class="line" id="230017"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#230017" label="[23:00:17]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message">well compression config should be obsolete, git comes with it's own gzip</span></div><div class="line" id="230220"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#230220" label="[23:02:20]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message">That said sometimes makes me feel like having a barebones implementation of git's protocol, because that one would work regardless of the forge and be more easily verifiable.</span></div><div class="line" id="230242"><span class="nick" style="color:#bc80bd"><a href="#230242" label="[23:02:42]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">that would be most ideal</span></div><div class="line" id="230257"><span class="nick" style="color:#3c5b35"><a href="#230257" label="[23:02:57]">&lt;deesix&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Git is not the only thing out there.</span></div><div class="line" id="230342"><span class="nick" style="color:#bc80bd"><a href="#230342" label="[23:03:42]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">it's what everything live-bootstrap cares about uses</span></div><div class="line" id="231817"><span class="nick" style="color:#bc80bd"><a href="#231817" label="[23:18:17]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Googulator: when you have a minute, what do you think about changing gnulib to getting sources from <a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/coreutils/gnulib?">https://github.com/coreutils/gnulib?</a>  we'd need to reupload all the gnulib tarballs to files.bootstrapping.world. (cgit for gnulib has disabled .tar.gz downloads :\)</span></div><div class="line" id="232719"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#232719" label="[23:27:19]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yeah git isn't alone but well… the others are much nicher and I guess could be bootstrappable from tarball/git fine (and I say this with packaging few things using cvs, mercurial and had bzr at one point)</span></div><div class="line" id="235207"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#235207" label="[23:52:07]">&lt;lanodan&gt;</a></span><span class="message">In fact checking live-bootstrap which I guess has the most extensive bootstrap steps, a `grep -v git steps/*/sources` seems to only show ones that are likely static archives.</span></div><br /></div></body></html>